Instead of installing an application directly on the operating system, containers run the application together with its libraries and settings as a packaged unit.
In this task, I built a Docker image by adding my own HTML page on top of the httpd:2.4 base image.

The workflow was:
docker build → docker run → docker ps → curl → verify the result.
Through this process, I learned how a service works when an image is executed as a container.

AWS ECR (Elastic Container Registry)
Rather than keeping the Docker image only on my local environment, I uploaded it to ECR, which makes it deployable and accessible in the cloud.

Managing the container lifecycle
I checked the running containers in the list, stopped and removed them, then verified the image list and demonstrated how to delete an image.

The need for a repository
By uploading local images to ECR, they can be shared with teammates and pulled into managed services such as ECS or EKS.

Concepts learned

Docker: a container platform that packages applications, libraries, and settings into an image, which can then be executed as a container.

Docker Image: a reusable template (a blueprint). It remains available even if the container is stopped or deleted. Images are built from Dockerfiles.

Docker Container: a running instance of an image (like a house built from a blueprint).

ECR: a Docker image repository provided by AWS for storing, versioning, and deploying container images.

# (1) Set environment variables — avoid typos by reusing these in all commands
export SID=s4083552
export REGION=us-east-1
export ACCOUNT_ID=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

# (2) Create a working directory and move into it — keep all Task A files organized here
mkdir -p ~/taskA && cd ~/taskA

# (3) Create the HTML file — this will be served by the Apache container
# It must include your name and student ID
cat > ${SID}.html <<EOF
<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><title>${SID}</title>
<h1>Hyuna Bae - ${SID}</h1></html>
EOF

# (4) Create the Dockerfile — base image httpd:2.4, copy HTML to Apache doc root, expose port 80
cat > Dockerfile <<'EOF'
FROM httpd:2.4
COPY s1234567.html /usr/local/apache2/htdocs/
EXPOSE 80
EOF

# (5) Replace placeholder student ID with the actual one in the Dockerfile
sed -i "s/s4083552/${SID}/g" Dockerfile

# (6) Build the Docker image — tag it with your student ID
docker build -t ${SID} .

# (7) Run the container — background mode (-d), map host port 8080 to container port 80, name: studentID_1
docker run -d -p 8080:80 --name ${SID}_1 ${SID}

# (8) List running containers — verify the container name and port mapping
docker ps

# (9) Test with curl — should return the HTML with your name and student ID
curl -s http://localhost:8080/${SID}.html

# (10) Create an ECR repository — name must follow format ecr-studentID-repo
aws ecr create-repository --repository-name ecr-${SID}-repo --region ${REGION}

# (11) Authenticate Docker with ECR — required before pushing images
aws ecr get-login-password --region ${REGION} \
 | docker login --username AWS --password-stdin ${ACCOUNT_ID}.dkr.ecr.${REGION}.amazonaws.com

# (12) Tag the image for ECR — link local image with the ECR repository URI
docker tag ${SID}:latest ${ACCOUNT_ID}.dkr.ecr.${REGION}.amazonaws.com/ecr-${SID}-repo:latest

# (13) Push the image to ECR — output should end with a digest line (sha256:…)
docker push ${ACCOUNT_ID}.dkr.ecr.${REGION}.amazonaws.com/ecr-${SID}-repo:latest

# (14) List all containers — show running and stopped containers before cleanup
docker ps -a

# (15) Stop and remove the container — cleanup process
CID=$(docker ps -aq --filter "name=${SID}_1"); docker stop "$CID"; docker rm "$CID"

# (16) Confirm container removal — list should no longer show the container
docker ps -a

# (17) List all local images
docker images

# (18) (Optional) Show the command to remove the image — required by assignment spec
# docker rmi ${SID}:latest
